      "text": "To determine the month when Hal will change the batteries for the 25th time, we need to calculate the cumulative time in months from the first change in May. Since a month has 5 months between changes, we can use modulo arithmetic to find the remainder when 25 is divided by the number of months between changes.\n\nHere's the step-by-step reasoning:\n\n1. The first change is in May, which is the 5th month.\n2. The second change will be in the 10th month (5 * 2 = 10).\n3. The third change will be in the 15th month (5 * 3 = 15).\n4. This pattern continues, so the \\(n\\)-th change will be in the \\(5(n-1)\\)-th month.\n\nFor the 25th change, we need to calculate:\n\\[ 5(25-1) = 5 \\times 24 = 120 \\]\nWe need to find the month in the 120th month of the calendar. Since each month is 5 months long, we can find the position within a complete cycle of 5 months by taking the modulo of 120 with 5:\n\\[ 120 \\mod 5 = 0 \\]\nThis means that after 120 months, it will be exactly the same month as it was originally.\n\nSince the first change was in May, the 25th change will also be in May. Therefore, the month when the batteries will be changed for the 25th time is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{May}}\n\\]\n\nLet's confirm this with a Python code snippet.\n```python\n# The first change is in May, which is the 5th month.\n# The nth change will be in the 5 * (n - 1) - 1 + 1 (since the changes are inclusive) month.\n# We need to find the 25th change, which is in the 5 * (25 - 1) + 1 = 120th month.\n# The 120th month will be the same month as the 0th month (mod 5), which is May (5th month).\n# Let's confirm this using a Python calculation.\nchange_month = 5 * (25 - 1) + 1\nprint(change_month)\n```",
