      "text": "To determine the month in which Hal will change the calculator batteries for the 25th time, we need to calculate the total duration in months between the first and the 25th replacement. Since Hal changes the batteries every 5 months, we can express the total number of months as a multiple of 5 plus any remainder.\n\nFirst, calculate the total number of months between the first replacement in May and the 25th replacement:\n\\[ 25 \\times 5 = 125 \\text{ months} \\]\n\nNext, we need to find out what month 125 months after May will fall. We do this by dividing 125 by 12 (since there are 12 months in a year) and considering the remainder:\n\\[ 125 \\div 12 = 10 \\text{ remainder } 5 \\]\n\nThis means that 125 months is equivalent to 10 years and 5 months. To find the month, we add 5 months to May:\n\\[ 5 + 5 = 10 \\text{ (which is October)} \\]\n\nTherefore, the batteries will be changed for the 25th time in \\boxed{October}.",
